{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization","id":1532266,"title":"Comprehensive list of Mage Organization?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-03-08T17:32:50-08:00","id":1532266,"title":"Comprehensive list of Mage Organization?","content":"I wanted to know how many and what kind of Mage Organization are there in Ascension.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28270-yumlum","name":"Yumlum","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-08T20:48:56-08:00","id":1532272,"title":"","content":"That's somewhat impossible. While the Traditions and the Technocracy are simply enough to identify and even list out their major factions (and even then getting into further sub-factions gets harder to be exhaustive with as such cliques can come and go over time), the rest of the mages aren't so easy to.<br /> <br /> There is a large list of Crafts that have been in the books, some of which are only mentioned by name, but they are also explicitly non-exhaustive lists. Crafts can range from groups on part with a Tradition or Convention (in fact a few Crafts are former Traditions, and there are Crafts that are splinter factions of Conventions that didn't opt to join the Traditions when the Union shoved them out). Even the M20 addition of the Disparate Alliance leaves the exact current membership up to the ST, with only a few of the groups essential as the founding members, and a lot of the rest as optional secondary or tertiary recruits.<br /> <br /> The Marauders and the Nephandi are more of a metaphysical classification, with no central organizing body. While both groups default to a more individualist modality, some are cooperative. Nephandi especially, since they have some distinct beliefs to differ over and thus form factions over, do have organizations, but purposefully avoid anything too concrete. If they were too easy to classify and identify for a comprehensive list, they'd be too easy for their enemies to notice their activities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-10T07:13:18-07:00","id":1532324,"title":"","content":"One of the problems of WoD is the unfathomable number of small &quot;crafts&quot;. That was already present even in VtM, but in MtA it is blown out of proportion. I remember seeing, I think that it was in the 2ed, that the proportion of Awaken towards Sleepers range in 1 to a million. That's... Problematic, because it would give us the amazing total of almost 8.000 &quot;Mages&quot; over the entire span of the globe. And that's with the population of today, not with the 500 millions humans that was the norm throughout the last 5000 years, or the 1 billion at the beginning of the 19th century or the 2 billions at the end of the 19 century.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Vampires would range around 80.000. A bit more decent, but once you start dividing those 80k between 13 clans and bloodlines, and then you divide again between sects, and then divide those between the internal &quot;groups&quot; inside these clans (the Tremere for instance used to have several different &quot;factions&quot; previous to the 5ed), then the numbers stop making sense.<br /> <br /> Let's do a quick thought exercise here: so, 8000 Mages, let's divide them between Technocracy, Traditions, Nephandi and Marauders AND the Crafts (Disparate Alliance or just &quot;Independent&quot;). Now, I would say that there probably are more Techies than Traditionalists (given that they are the top dogs), and possibly more Trads than crafts, and probably the numbers of Nephandis and Marauders is way smaller, but let us divide equally just as part of this thought experiment.<br /> <br /> Let us also be generous and up the number to 10.000 to have more round numbers. So, that would give 2.000 &quot;Mages&quot; to each one of the &quot;factions&quot;.<br /> <br /> So, 2.000 Technocrats, divided by 5 Conventions, that gives 400 Enlightened Scientists to each Convention. Now, I don't know about you but, if my Iteration X had only 400 Enlightened personnel, I wouldn't be throwing them out by turning them into Cyborgs to do a Hit Mark's job. Neither would I be throwing agents out in dangerous missions for Men in Black, nor would I be throwing out those precious few Enlightened as mere conscripts in Deep Space to fight off &quot;Things-men-were-not-meant-to-know&quot;.<br /> <br /> No, every single one of those would be inside a highly secure lab, doing lab work and not much else.<br /> <br /> Now, some Conventions have up to 5 internal groups, which gives the staggering number of 80 people to each.<br /> <br /> It gets worse: the Trads have 9 Traditions; lets include the Orphans as number 10, also for rounding sake, and that gives us the amazing number of 200 Mages to each; and let's not forget that this is just a rounded number, because in reality, some would have more than others, meaning that some traditions (the Verbena, Etherites and VA and perhaps the Ectastics being the better examples) would have less, possibly not even up to 100 members.<br /> <br /> Then you divide those 200 members of the Order of Hermes between at least a dozen houses, and all of the sudden the &quot;feared&quot; House of Flambeau is less of a &quot;House&quot; and more of a Chantry with 20 members (if that much).<br /> <br /> Then you go to the Crafts and you try to divide 2000 individuals between hundreds of different small groups, and you may well end up with a &quot;Craft&quot; that is made up of a single individual...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude02","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-10T08:07:19-07:00","id":1532326,"title":"","content":"Given that, if you have two mages you have three irreconcilable opinions, there would be more mages organizations and suborganizations than visible stars in the desert sky. There are always more mage organizations, most of them totally trivial, until they aren't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-10T09:10:55-07:00","id":1532330,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1532326#post1532326\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given that, if you have two mages you have three irreconcilable opinions, there would be more mages organizations and suborganizations than visible stars in the desert sky. There are always more mage organizations, most of them totally trivial, until they aren't.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, pretty much. So much so that each small chantry of 3 mages could be considered it's own &quot;organization&quot;. Most of whom will wither and die as soon as their members do, or as soon as they disperse.<br /> <br /> Once in a while though, they may shake the very foundations of the Earth.<br /> <br /> Christianism for instance, started with a guy preaching to 13 of his disciples... But eventually became the official religion of the most powerful empire in the world at the time, and now is the largest religion in the world.<br /> <br /> But, for every &quot;Christianism&quot; there arises in the world, there's countless secret societies of occultism that disappears after a few years...<br /> <br /> I've actually witnessed this myself. I'm an atheist, but my wife is &quot;Umbandista&quot;, a Brazilian religion of African origin, for those of the US, it is very similar to Voodoo or Santeria, it believes in spirits of the ancestors and has become highly syncretical with Christianism.<br /> <br /> I participated in a few sessions, which I absolutely HATE, just like I hate going to Catholic or Evangelical church's, because I'm a second generation atheist therefore &quot;going to church&quot; was never part of my life, thus I find ANY kind of religious rituals to be utterly boring, but I've been to quite a few number of churches (because of a former Christian girlfriend) and to the &quot;Umbanda terreiros&quot; (the name for the &quot;temples&quot; of that religion), and what it is clearly evident is that each church, mostly the evangelical ones, is slightly different from one another, just like each one of the &quot;temples&quot; of Umbanda have their own particular idiosyncrasies that are reflected on the emphasis they give on certain practices that are peculiar to each one. There's no one monolithic &quot;evangelicals&quot; or &quot;umbandists&quot; or not even Catholics (which is a more centralized religion, probably more than any other in the world).<br /> <br /> I've also witnessed several temples and churches closing and ending their operations, while new ones are open.<br /> <br /> So yes, small organizations of Mages are constantly popping in and out of existence constantly, and not just because their members fall victims of the Ascension War; people simply change. My older step daughter started to date an evangelical boy, and now she's singing praise songs everyday, despite her mother following a pagan religion and me being an &quot;apostate&quot;; people constantly go from Mormon temples to &quot;New Age&quot; esoteric groups online and so on. So, those organizations are formed, sometimes a member goes out to find something else different, others get in, and so it goes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-10T15:46:58-07:00","id":1532352,"title":"","content":"Or we just ignore the poorly thought out idea of trying to tie the number of mages that exist at a time to the number of humans; just like the books did.<br /> <br /> Even the whole &quot;1:100K&quot; vampire to human thing is something Vampire moved from &quot;true&quot; to &quot;Camarilla ideal that is rarely strictly adhered to, and nobody else actually cares about.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-13T14:56:44-07:00","id":1535532,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1532352#post1532352\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or we just ignore the poorly thought out idea of trying to tie the number of mages that exist at a time to the number of humans; just like the books did.<br /> <br /> Even the whole &quot;1:100K&quot; vampire to human thing is something Vampire moved from &quot;true&quot; to &quot;Camarilla ideal that is rarely strictly adhered to, and nobody else actually cares about.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>While that's good, I actually don't think the small number of Mages is an actual problem.<br /> <br /> This is more explored in the Union (Extraordinary Citizens) and Crafts (Hedge Magicians), but Mage in general doesn't need a lot of actual Awakened to work.<br /> <br /> As I see it, most of the work for any faction comes solely from Sleepers with any degree of understanding about the group. In fact, some groups can easily survive for years or even centuries without a single Awakened in activity.<br /> <br /> Mages are exceedingly idiosyncratic and resourceful, even in the Technocratic Union. We have a certain bias to think that &quot;low level characters&quot; of the type of the PCs are the bulk of their organizations and workforce, but actually any Mage is more likely to be the feverish researcher or super agent behind Big projects and missions, instead of the bulk of anything.<br /> <br /> On top of that, Mage games are more likely to cover more ground, going places, to involve NPCs from all around the world. So you don't need a high density to have your PCs meeting a lot of Mages, they're more likely to meet most Mages in existence at some point.<br /> <br /> I only think an exception may be needed in the case of the Umbra and its many realms. But for that I just assume that the 8k number is for Earth residents.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-13T16:09:07-07:00","id":1535535,"title":"","content":"I think that while Magnus's argument his a bit hyperbolic, it still fundamentally correct: the number of factions in Mage is very high for the implied rarity of them. While mages are certainly capable of a lot of globetrotting (though edition matters a lot), the books never present your average mage as knowing most mages even within their own factions. The Order of Hermes basically differentiates between the major Houses and minor Houses by this very metric. A major House within the larger order is big enough where you probably don't know all of the other members of your House. A House that small is what makes up the minor Houses that join together to form House Ex Misc which is supposed to be numerically on par with the major Houses.<br /> <br /> Even padding things out with consors and so on, what the books present isn't cohesive. There's too many Chantries, and too many factions, that require Awakened mages of a certain percentage of the groups, for mages to be that rare.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-13T23:13:30-07:00","id":1535543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535535#post1535535\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that while Magnus's argument his a bit hyperbolic, it still fundamentally correct: the number of factions in Mage is very high for the implied rarity of them. While mages are certainly capable of a lot of globetrotting (though edition matters a lot), the books never present your average mage as knowing most mages even within their own factions. The Order of Hermes basically differentiates between the major Houses and minor Houses by this very metric. A major House within the larger order is big enough where you probably don't know all of the other members of your House. A House that small is what makes up the minor Houses that join together to form House Ex Misc which is supposed to be numerically on par with the major Houses.<br /> <br /> Even padding things out with consors and so on, what the books present isn't cohesive. There's too many Chantries, and too many factions, that require Awakened mages of a certain percentage of the groups, for mages to be that rare.</div> </div> </div> </div>That depends on the size of a chantry. For me a small one is 2-3 mages. A medium one 5 and a really big one 10-15. Thats small enough to fit the bill. As for knowing people. Do you know your neihbours? In high school did you know every member of other classes (as in the ones youre not forced to interact with)?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T05:03:19-07:00","id":1535553,"title":"","content":"House ruling the numbers well below what the books have described really doesn't make a strong case that the books are fine as written. Doissetep, which is certainly meant to represent the upper tiers of what a Chantry could be, had ten <b>cabals</b> of mages, and hundreds of un-Awakened support types, with the main Chantry in a Shard Realm, three physical Chantry houses on Earth, and a dozen addition Nodes it was connected to. &quot;10-15&quot; mages cannot manage something like that. Most of the Traditions, the Council of Nine combined in Horizon, and each Technocratic Convention were all supposed to have at least one Chantry/Construct/etc. of that scale (at least in 2e before the Avatar Storm). There have been almost 30 Tradition controlled Chantries detailed in the books over the years, and the small ones of those are generally 20-30 members (since they're supposed to be fully flushed out places with active story hooks for PCs to visit and do stuff at), while there's also supposed to be a multitude of smaller ones controlled by 1-3 Cabals out there as well.<br /> <br /> The numbers, again, don't add up for mages being literally one in a million. There's just too much stuff going on in the setting.<br /> <br /> And I went to a high-school that was small enough that there was no such thing as &quot;other classes.&quot; Everyone in the same grade level had at least a few classes that overlapped with everyone else. It's not hard to know everyone when it's only ~100 people.<br /> <br /> But that's exactly the problem. Either mage groups are so small they can't have the cohesion the books say they have, or they're big enough to support the social organizations in the game, which means there need to be a lot more individual mages. In my tiny high school, we didn't have a lot of the &quot;cliques&quot; people imagine because there just wasn't enough people to splinter off into their own little social circles and never really interact with anyone outside of it. I was the dorkiest loner gamer nerd stereotype possible, but I was also a yearbook editor, a lead crew member for plays and musicals, one of the athletics equipment manages (though in all fairness the drama crew and the sports team had to share storage space anyway), while being part of a gaming club, a computer club (the video gamers), and a literary club. Because &quot;hey we need more people to make this happen, doesn't that guy like this stuff even if he's kinda weird? Lets get him.&quot; overrode social boundaries. It's not that groups didn't exist, but the lines were blurry and amorphous. We couldn't have jocks, and preps, and nerd, and stoners, and all those other stereotypes as separate groups because if we actually broke down to that granular of a level, the groups would all be too small to actually do anything. <br /> <br /> And that's the tension in how Mage has been written. There are too many groups for the rarity the books have described, such that the books clearly ignore their own rarity guidelines all the time because otherwise the setting doesn't work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T06:26:58-07:00","id":1535560,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535553#post1535553\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And I went to a high-school that was small enough that there was no such thing as &quot;other classes.&quot; Everyone in the same grade level had at least a few classes that overlapped with everyone else. It's not hard to know everyone when it's only ~100 people.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>By other class i ment other group not a place. I had a small school aswell and i dont recall knowning more than 3 people from outside my class group.<br /> <br /> As for a possible explanation. There could be a lot of immortal mages that cant go to the material plane (for long) anymore cause of paradox.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28439,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","user":"quelsec","date":". 05-14-2024, 09:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T12:59:27-07:00","id":1535581,"title":"","content":"I\u2019ve always had a similar problem to what Heavy Arms is describing above, which applies to basically every gameline in WoD on some level (except maybe Wraith) but I\u2019ve always felt that it was a particularly extreme case. For all the reasons listen above and the plentiful crafts, factions, &amp; groups listed in the books you need to have some solid numbers but that also seems to directly run against the rarity of mages and what I think is meant to be their vibe of each being 1 in a million will-workers with the potential to vastly alter the world. Mages are perhaps the gameline I\u2019d be most inclined to play with as limited as possible interaction with its splat social structure, with every mage going their own way (or their cabal\u2019s own way). <br /> <br /> But the problem is, how do you get the sheer numbers for the factions and crafts the books describe. Even if you limit the numbers to just crafts and traditions it still is a pretty huge scarcity. If one wants to run mages like a vast underground of more or less remote nodes scattered across the world like sorcerers in Hellblazer or the gods in American Gods, that makes sense and could work but it doesn\u2019t line up with the density of social activity the books describe.<br /> <br /> Something I\u2019ve toyed with is maybe blurring the line between True Mages (Awakened) and sorcerers (and numinae users, etc.) and having larger numbers of sorcerers, with perhaps the capacity for them to elevate to mage-hood themselves or participate in rituals led by true mages, and using these sorcerers to fill out the ranks of magical organizations and form a resource (like nodes, etc.) which true mages can compete over to bolster their own organization. I dunno, just spitballing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T13:14:57-07:00","id":1535586,"title":"","content":"It does solve the problem if a lot of the Traditions, Conventions, and Crafts membership is made up of unawakened, but it also has some unintended consequences. It will often cast mages as the select few who know The Truth, while everyone else is just a support. That fits well for some secret societies and mystery cults (which is part of why it works better in Awakening), but it is a poor fit in more mainstream religious organizations and scientific societies and the like.<br /> <br /> That said, I can see most of the Traditions at least as sleeper societies that mage members either have a disproportionate influence on or at least get a lot of support from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T13:26:11-07:00","id":1535588,"title":"","content":"I must say i dont really like Sorcerers. I think there redundant. Whats the purpuse of half baked mages? But yeah we could assume that many mages have followers.<br /> Still i prefer to think that mages are rare. The chantries are 2-15 members max and the traditions keep connected via magical skype (your empire can be as large as your ability of communication allows).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T15:48:02-07:00","id":1535595,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535586#post1535586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It does solve the problem if a lot of the Traditions, Conventions, and Crafts membership is made up of unawakened, but it also has some unintended consequences. It will often cast mages as the select few who know The Truth, while everyone else is just a support. That fits well for some secret societies and mystery cults (which is part of why it works better in Awakening), but it is a poor fit in more mainstream religious organizations and scientific societies and the like.<br /> <br /> That said, I can see most of the Traditions at least as sleeper societies that mage members either have a disproportionate influence on or at least get a lot of support from.</div> </div> </div> </div>In a &quot;most are Sleepers&quot; scenario it doesn't have to mean that Mages are the only ones to know The Truth\u2122, but the opposite, most of the people involved in everything in the setting are Consors, maybe even most Cabals are composed more of skilled Hedge Magicians, with just one or two actual Awakened.<br /> <br /> And I think it works, although I agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> that this is more homebrewing the problem away than the books being secretly coherent.<br /> <br /> Mages are disproportionately influential. But most of the day to day work in any Chantry needs just common skills to manage the place, maybe the ability to use some key Wonders, and minor magical abilities below the full range of an Awakened.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T03:05:03-07:00","id":1535622,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>quelsec</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535560#post1535560\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By other class i ment other group not a place.</div> </div> </div> </div>So did I.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I had a small school aswell and i dont recall knowning more than 3 people from outside my class group. </div> </div> </div>We didn't have &quot;class groups.&quot; The kids I had English and history classes with were a different mix than who I had STEM (not that it was called that back then) with, and who I did secondary language with, and who I did art with, and PE with, etc. Which meant we inevitable interacted with most of our grade level every day (before even getting into extracurriculars).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for a possible explanation. There could be a lot of immortal mages that cant go to the material plane (for long) anymore cause of paradox. </div> </div> </div>Immortality is, generally, not really possible even if living a really long time is. But that just expands how many mages there are in the setting because mages keep Awakening and then have the potential to live a few centuries. If just 50 mages of those that Awaken every year make it to the &quot;live 200 years&quot; level of power, that means there's around 10K of just those running around. Which would make them all of the mages if we took the &quot;one in a million&quot; thing literally, and it would still mean that most of the factions in the game wouldn't find a mage of that talent more than once every few years.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>quelsec</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535588#post1535588\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Whats the purpuse of half baked mages?</div> </div> </div> </div>Since they don't cause Paradox and are easier to train (unlike mages they don't need to Awaken, a process mages can't just &quot;make&quot; happen) by tapping into Sleepers that are already aligned with a given group of mage's beliefs. Sorcerer's help generate and maintain Reality Zones because they prime normal Sleepers into accepting belief systems in ways the power of Awakened magic can't because said Sleepers would Disbelief away the more potent magic of full mages.<br /> <br /> Many mages also find cultivating sorcerers, beyond the ready supply of useful followers, the best way to recruit new mages as some sorcerers Awaken as well, and freshly Awakened mages looking for answers are likely to gravitated towards the same methods of recruiting new sorcerer students.<br /> <br /> Though it seems worth pointing out that when sorcery Paths were first introduced to the WoD, they were not affiliated with Mage. Even after sorcerers became mostly associated with Mage as a game-line, lots of sorcerers aren't even aware of mages or their social organizations. Whether they work independently, or in their own groups, or as part of other supernatural aware groups (a lot of the Hunters Hunted orgs have sorcerers in their ranks). Sorcery is also just mechanically easier than Awakened magic, so it appeals to people that want to play in the Mage setting but find the Sphere system too much to have fun with an prefer the more straightforward design of Numina based characters.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Still i prefer to think that mages are rare. The chantries are 2-15 members max and the traditions keep connected via magical skype (your empire can be as large as your ability of communication allows). </div> </div> </div>Since you don't seem to be contending that this isn't how the books are presenting things... why are you repeating it? You do you. We're not trying to tell you how to run the game, we're trying to explain what the problem in the base version of the game is that we're talking about.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535581#post1535581\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I\u2019ve always had a similar problem to what Heavy Arms is describing above, which applies to basically every gameline in WoD on some level (except maybe Wraith) but I\u2019ve always felt that it was a particularly extreme case.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this gets overstated, or at least a lot of the other game-lines handled it better.<br /> <br /> VtM and Mage suffer the most from the problem.<br /> <br /> WtA kinda does, but then just embraced it in the writing: werewolves used to be more than plentiful enough to fill out all of these different grous and sub-groups, but most of them are a shadow of what they used to be thanks to the impending Apocalypse. The Children of Gaia just up and tried to get rid of their Camps for a bit because Camps are a waste of time and resources, while the Silver Fangs and Black Furies are noted as having less and less younger Garou buy into their age-old social groups that used to run the Tribes. Storytellers are advised to build Caerns with far less NPCs needed to fill all the tradition Sept roles to both give PCs ways to get into the social hierarchy and stress how dire the situation really is.<br /> <br /> CtD only really has this problem for the Sidhe and all of their Houses, because otherwise the rarity of different types of them is offset by most of them being regional, and they tend to claim giant areas as single governing units rather than try to stuff everything into one city or similar territory.<br /> <br /> All of the Revised additional games basically followed WtA and worked the &quot;ideal&quot; numbers being far off from the in-game reality as part of the drama of the games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Something I\u2019ve toyed with is maybe blurring the line between True Mages (Awakened) and sorcerers (and numinae users, etc.) and having larger numbers of sorcerers, with perhaps the capacity for them to elevate to mage-hood themselves or participate in rituals led by true mages, and using these sorcerers to fill out the ranks of magical organizations and form a resource (like nodes, etc.) which true mages can compete over to bolster their own organization. I dunno, just spitballing. </div> </div> </div>That's basically already what Mage did in late 2e and Revised; and it still leaves the issue of the thread present in those books (one of the reasons I felt another poster's presentation was correct by hyperbolic). As I mentioned, Doissetep has hundreds of un-Awakened people living there (most of them were probably sorcerers, but the write up of the Chantry came before the normalization of sorcerer as the most common kind of Consor).<br /> <br /> The idea was always that the fully Awakened mages were sitting on the top of factions with lots of aware-but-not-mage folks bellow them to help keep everything running.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535595#post1535595\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">..maybe even most Cabals are composed more of skilled Hedge Magicians, with just one or two actual Awakened.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is something in fairly stark contrast to how the books have written things. One of a few groups in the setting would have mixed cabals like this where sorcerers were treated as equals rather than support characters. While PC cabals are special and unique and can make this work, you'd have to rewrite the official stances of most of the factions in the setting to make this normal instead of a rarity among already rare groups.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mages are disproportionately influential. But most of the day to day work in any Chantry needs just common skills to manage the place, maybe the ability to use some key Wonders, and minor magical abilities below the full range of an Awakened. </div> </div> </div>I think that vastly depends on the Chantry in question. A 1-2 dot Chantry most certainly operates in a fashion where most Chantry needs could be done by Sleepers if managed properly, or acolytes with no specific talents. Once you get into 3+ dot Chantries, more and more requires at least a few dots in Numina to be useful enough to keep tings running right instead of being ritual components; and even then there are going to be things that even sorcerers with the highest stats would struggle to just maintain while only an Awakened mage could really do so for the long term.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T05:33:48-07:00","id":1535635,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535622#post1535622\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So did I.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> We didn't have &quot;class groups.&quot; The kids I had English and history classes with were a different mix than who I had STEM (not that it was called that back then) with, and who I did secondary language with, and who I did art with, and PE with, etc. Which meant we inevitable interacted with most of our grade level every day (before even getting into extracurriculars).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Immortality is, generally, not really possible even if living a really long time is. But that just expands how many mages there are in the setting because mages keep Awakening and then have the potential to live a few centuries. If just 50 mages of those that Awaken every year make it to the &quot;live 200 years&quot; level of power, that means there's around 10K of just those running around. Which would make them all of the mages if we took the &quot;one in a million&quot; thing literally, and it would still mean that most of the factions in the game wouldn't find a mage of that talent more than once every few years.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>But that still makes sense. If mages live in small chantries they have no business knowing anyone outside of it. They could be friendly with some on the outside but thats not the rule. The social structure between the chantries is kept via communication devices for defencive and trade purpuses and thats it. The traditions are more of like sword schools in japan. They exist cause they train people in their way.<br /> <br /> I admit i do not really get why there would have to be many mages to keep chantries going. Set defences, harvest quintesence and do your own experiments. Thats it. The chantries idea comes from Ars Magica and still is a good example how Hermes chantries operate. There is nothing that would require a lot of mages.<br /> <br /> By the numbers if mage is one in a milion then with a 8bilion population we have 8000 mages. Lets say the technocracy has 2x more than the traditions. thats still more than 2 thousand members. Devided by 9 traditions and orphans thats 250+ mages per tradition. For complex social interactions on organisation level to take place you need only about 50 people (the size of a tribal hunting party). Distance does not matter cause telepathy and teleport so i would say its fine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28439,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","user":"quelsec","date":". 05-15-2024, 11:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T14:30:37-07:00","id":1535676,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>quelsec</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535635#post1535635\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If mages live in small chantries they have no business knowing anyone outside of it. They could be friendly with some on the outside but thats not the rule. The social structure between the chantries is kept via communication devices for defencive and trade purpuses and thats it.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is inherently self-contradictory though. If most mages live in small Chantries and rarely if ever met mages from other places... why do they have a magical mass communication and travel system that keeps them all connected to each other and enables large scale social organizations?<br /> <br /> You don't get cities and civilizations which then have lots of internal subdivisions when everyone is still living in small hunter-gatherer bands that barely interact with each other.<br /> <br /> Without some critical mass of population, there's no reason for mages to form groups other then small local ones. There's no way to achieve the common philosophy, cause, and social identity that drives trusting other members of national or global scale institutions without numbers first. &quot;Defense and trade&quot; is something that scales up with community size. If a &quot;big&quot; group of mages is 15 individuals, you don't need a global defense pact, you just need a few locals that will band together to push away any large intrusions and then go back to barely talking to each other. Trade is also something that goes up exponentially with population. Small Chantries can't produce much to offer in trade, so trade is going to be a small and limited affair that is, again, mostly local. Trade requires the ability to produce in excess of what you need (that is to make stuff you can afford to trade away to others). 100 people working together towards a common goal are going to produce more than ten groups of 10 individuals will on their own. Whether it's grain, or it's knowledge, you need to gather people together in scale to increase the rate of output and enable meaningful scaled trade.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I admit i do not really get why there would have to be many mages to keep chantries going. Set defences, harvest quintesence and do your own experiments. Thats it. The chantries idea comes from Ars Magica and still is a good example how Hermes chantries operate. There is nothing that would require a lot of mages. </div> </div> </div>Mage might have taken a lot of inspiration from AM, but they differ enough that you can't really compare the two like this. One of the big differences is the Ascension War. The whole &quot;set defenses&quot; part is a much larger investment when you're fending off a squad of VE Awakened marines, backed by a few fireteams of enlightened soldiers, and a couple of combat HITMarks, instead of peasant revolts, witch-hunters, and challenges of one-on-one magical duels.<br /> <br /> The Order of Reason went with scale, because scale is way more powerful, and the Traditions had to form and adapt to that reality. Mystic magic, for lack of a better phrase, had to industrialize to avoid getting steamrolled. What you're talking about with small groups of mages and all that, is <i>history</i> in Ascension. That's how things used to be before the Mythic Age ended and things changed and forced groups to change with it. The era of wizards sitting in their towers with some servants doing their little experiments ended when mages started not just fighting each other over various things, but having full scale - Sleeper involved - wars with each other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> By the numbers if mage is one in a milion then with a 8bilion population we have 8000 mages. Lets say the technocracy has 2x more than the traditions. thats still more than 2 thousand members. Devided by 9 traditions and orphans thats 250+ mages per tradition. For complex social interactions on organisation level to take place you need only about 50 people (the size of a tribal hunting party). Distance does not matter cause telepathy and teleport so i would say its fine. </div> </div> </div>OK, but you completely left out the Crafts/Disparates, Marauders, and Nephandi from your math, and haven't addressed all the subfactions within the Traditions.<br /> <br /> Also, the assertion that &quot;50&quot; people is enough for &quot;complex social interactions on organizational levels&quot; is something you're going to have to back up with more than just a claim of that being the size of a tribal hunting party. The phrase &quot;tribal hunting party&quot; by itself already undermines the notion because that's just part of a larger social entity already. That group is not performing social interactions in a vacuum, it's bringing the social resources of the hundreds of people they are hunting on behalf of along with them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T15:20:27-07:00","id":1535683,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>quelsec</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535588#post1535588\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I must say i dont really like Sorcerers. I think there redundant. Whats the purpuse of half baked mages?</div> </div> </div> </div>To have plenty of NPCs with simpler powers, but still able to fulfill a lot of story roles you don't need or don't want a full fledged Mage.<br /> <br /> Why should I need a full Mage NPC just to be MagicGuard#2?<br /> <br /> Seriously, the only reason why the proportions problem isn't bigger in Mage than in Vampire is that it is easy to solve with Sorcerers. And that's precisely because Sorcerers can fill most of the same roles, something you can't do with Ghouls in Vampire.<br /> <br /> You can't simultaneously impose the 8k global number, with no significant number of Consors and no Sorcerers, and have the organizations, workforce and conflicts in the scale the setting describes. At least, not being coherent.<br /> <br /> Even the Umbra can only solve the conundrum to a certain degree. If you put too much of Mage happening out there, then it is a fairly different setting than the one described in the books. It may be your version and may be excellent, but it isn't the one in the actual books.<br /> <br /> Let's see, there are, 9 Traditions, 8 of them have at least 3 minor divisions within, the other is \u200bthe Order of Hermes, comprised of 8 Major Houses and 7 Ex-Miscelanea, then there are 5 Conventions with about 4 subdivisions each, then we have 10 big Crafts, at least one with two subdivisions, plus 8 minor Crafts and we still have Orphans. Those are, mind you, just the groups <i><b>confirmed</b></i> to be in activity by the time of M20 Core. That's 77 groups of relevance, plus unaffiliated. And those groups are meant to be big enough to be at war on a global scale.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T15:22:23-07:00","id":1535684,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535676#post1535676\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is inherently self-contradictory though. If most mages live in small Chantries and rarely if ever met mages from other places... why do they have a magical mass communication and travel system that keeps them all connected to each other and enables large scale social organizations?<br /> <br /> You don't get cities and civilizations which then have lots of internal subdivisions when everyone is still living in small hunter-gatherer bands that barely interact with each other.<br /> <br /> Without some critical mass of population, there's no reason for mages to form groups other then small local ones. There's no way to achieve the common philosophy, cause, and social identity that drives trusting other members of national or global scale institutions without numbers first. &quot;Defense and trade&quot; is something that scales up with community size. If a &quot;big&quot; group of mages is 15 individuals, you don't need a global defense pact, you just need a few locals that will band together to push away any large intrusions and then go back to barely talking to each other. Trade is also something that goes up exponentially with population. Small Chantries can't produce much to offer in trade, so trade is going to be a small and limited affair that is, again, mostly local. Trade requires the ability to produce in excess of what you need (that is to make stuff you can afford to trade away to others). 100 people working together towards a common goal are going to produce more than ten groups of 10 individuals will on their own. Whether it's grain, or it's knowledge, you need to gather people together in scale to increase the rate of output and enable meaningful scaled trade.</div> </div> </div> </div>Simple they have to organise to survive. Like the polis in Greece.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535582-alternate-otherworlds-structure?p=1535677#post1535677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mage might have taken a lot of inspiration from AM, but they differ enough that you can't really compare the two like this. One of the big differences is the Ascension War. The whole &quot;set defenses&quot; part is a much larger investment when you're fending off a squad of VE Awakened marines, backed by a few fireteams of enlightened soldiers, and a couple of combat HITMarks, instead of peasant revolts, witch-hunters, and challenges of one-on-one magical duels.<br /> <br /> The Order of Reason went with scale, because scale is way more powerful, and the Traditions had to form and adapt to that reality. Mystic magic, for lack of a better phrase, had to industrialize to avoid getting steamrolled. What you're talking about with small groups of mages and all that, is <i>history</i> in Ascension. That's how things used to be before the Mythic Age ended and things changed and forced groups to change with it. The era of wizards sitting in their towers with some servants doing their little experiments ended when mages started not just fighting each other over various things, but having full scale - Sleeper involved - wars with each other.</div> </div> </div> </div>The idea is technocrats are streached thin and infighting thats why those kinds of attecks are rare. In middle ages mages had to deffend agains dragons and oher mosnters so i would say its still about the same but more mundane.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1535340-w5-videogame-text-adventure-book-of-hungry-names-review-first-impressions?p=1535678#post1535678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK, but you completely left out the Crafts/Disparates, Marauders, and Nephandi from your math, and haven't addressed all the subfactions within the Traditions.<br /> <br /> Also, the assertion that &quot;50&quot; people is enough for &quot;complex social interactions on organizational levels&quot; is something you're going to have to back up with more than just a claim of that being the size of a tribal hunting party. The phrase &quot;tribal hunting party&quot; by itself already undermines the notion because that's just part of a larger social entity already. That group is not performing social interactions in a vacuum, it's bringing the social resources of the hundreds of people they are hunting on behalf of along with them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I left about 300+ in my calculations so they can fit there. Also i do not really count those that live in the umbra.<br /> 50 would be the average population of a small village in the antic. Villages that payed taxes, traded with other villages, defended themselves and often prospered. All that without magic.Those small human habitats were often isolated for long periods of time. Having their own costums and traditions original to those regions.<br /> Mages can operate smaller thanks to magic. As for the connections they form naturally. Masters train students and students wana stay in touch. They can stay in touch thanks to comunication magic and so the culture persists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T15:54:52-07:00","id":1535690,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>quelsec</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535684#post1535684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Simple they have to organise to survive. Like the polis in Greece.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The idea is technocrats are streached thin and infighting thats why those kinds of attecks are rare. In middle ages mages had to deffend agains dragons and oher mosnters so i would say its still about the same but more mundane.<br /> <br /> <br /> I left about 300+ in my calculations so they can fit there. Also i do not really count those that live in the umbra.<br /> 50 would be the average population of a small village in the antic. Villages that payed taxes, traded with other villages, defended themselves and often prospered. All that without magic.Those small human habitats were often isolated for long periods of time. Having their own costums and traditions original to those regions.<br /> Mages can operate smaller thanks to magic. As for the connections they form naturally. Masters train students and students wana stay in touch. They can stay in touch thanks to comunication magic and so the culture persists.</div> </div> </div> </div>Villages didn't existed on their own. They had close neighbors at the very least, and most had regular contact with a larger system of settlements. any villager was still part of a society with at least a few thousands even before calling it a kingdom.<br /> <br /> Either way, Mage societies don't translate to villages. Chantries, maybe. Villages didn't had, by themselves, all the systems and institutions a Mage society has, much less were able to go to large scale war by themselves.<br /> <br /> They didn't have even all the systems and institutions they needed just for their daily activities, a lot of basic services were spread through a large area with several villages as far as a day on foot from each other.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T16:43:21-07:00","id":1535692,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>quelsec</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1532266-comprehensive-list-of-mage-organization?p=1535684#post1535684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Simple they have to organise to survive. Like the polis in Greece.</div> </div> </div> </div>This feels like conceding the point without realizing it.<br /> <br /> A polis, while strict definitions are contested, was not a small village of hunter-gatherers where everyone knows each other. A polis was an organized society centered on a specific location that has grown enough to have a formal set of laws to manage governing the people Ancient writers considered the ability to send representatives to the Olympic games one of the essential milestones of becoming a polis: having a large enough society to have professional athletes.<br /> <br /> Polis as a metric means you're acknowledging that things need to hit a critical mass of population to form that level of society.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T17:01:36-07:00","id":1535694,"title":"","content":"A small polis had about 10k people if we include slaves (which we should).<br /> <br /> While it isn't just urban area, this population was well within walking distance of each other.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}